{:edb/db-before-t 812 :edb/db-after-t 813 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534125 50 #inst "2026-10-11T16:54:20.662Z" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186059687 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411060 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186059687 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411063 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186059688 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411059 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186059688 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411062 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186059688 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411064 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186059689 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411058 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186059689 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411065 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186059689 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411066 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186059690 1038 #edb/ref 17592186411061 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186059925 1050 540.0 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186060062 1068 #edb/ref 17592186056730 13194139534125 17592186045417 false] [17592186060139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057200 13194139534125 17592186045417 false] [17592186060139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411055 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186060139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186060139 1071 "$\\,$ If you don’t study, then you won't pass the exam.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186060139 1071 "If you don’t study, then you won't pass the exam.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" 13194139534125 17592186045417 false] [17592186060139 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You don't study.\n\n- $B:$ You won't pass the exam.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You study.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You study, or you won't pass the exam.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Study, or you won't pass the exam." 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186060140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057206 13194139534125 17592186045417 false] [17592186060143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057224 13194139534125 17592186045417 false] [17592186060984 1064 "The conditional statement $A \\Rightarrow B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $\\lnot A \\lor B{:}$\n\n$$A \\Rightarrow B \\equiv \\lnot A \\lor B$$\n\nWe can verify this by the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, A \\Rightarrow B \\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\lor B \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n\nIn other words, any conditional statement of the form\n\n$\\qquad$ *if antecedent, then consequent*\n\ncan be expressed equivalently as\n\n$\\qquad$ *not antecedent, or consequent.*\n\nFor example, consider the following conditional statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If you do not take your watch, then you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nLet's rewrite this statement using its logically equivalent form.\n\nFirst, we denote the antecedent $A$ and consequent $B$ as follows:\n\n- $A{:}$ You do not take your watch.\n- $B{:}$ You will be late for the meeting.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A \\Rightarrow B,$ which is logically equivalent to\n\n$$\\neg A \\lor B.$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ is:\n\n- $\\neg A{:}$ You take your watch.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ *You take your watch, or you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nWe can write this using more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *Take your watch, or you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nAnother way to write this is to append the word \"either\" to the beginning of the sentence, like so:\n\n$\\qquad$ *Either you take your watch, or you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nUsing the word \"either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(\\lnot A\\lor B)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." 13194139534125 17592186045417 false] [17592186060984 1064 "The conditional statement $A \\Rightarrow B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $\\lnot A \\lor B{:}$ \n\n$$\nA \\Rightarrow B \\equiv \\lnot A \\lor B\n$$\n\nWe can verify this by the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, A \\Rightarrow B \\,$ | $\\, \\neg A \\lor B \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n\nIn other words, any conditional statement of the form\n\n$\\qquad$ *if antecedent, then consequent*\n\ncan be expressed equivalently as \n\n$\\qquad$ *not antecedent, or consequent.*\n\nFor example, consider the following conditional statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If you do not take your watch, then you will be late for the meeting.* \n\nLet's rewrite this statement using its logically equivalent form. \n\nFirst, we denote the antecedent $A$ and consequent $B$ as follows:  \n\n- $A{:}$ You do not take your watch.\n\n- $B{:}$ You will be late for the meeting.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A \\Rightarrow B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\neg A \\lor B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ is:\n\n- $\\neg A{:}$ You take your watch.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ *You take your watch, or you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nWe can write this using more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ *Take your watch, or you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nAnother way to write this is to append the word \"either\" to the beginning of the sentence, like so:\n\n$\\qquad$ *Either you take your watch, or you will be late for the meeting.*\n\nUsing the word \"either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(\\lnot A\\lor B)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186410998 1080 "Either you follow the recipe, or the dish will be delicious." 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186410998 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186410998 1083 #uuid "031baea5-6ff9-513e-b2ec-02a90e898fe5" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186410999 1080 "If you don't win this game, then you will make it to the playoffs." 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186410999 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186410999 1083 #uuid "05f48d79-1191-5d91-bccd-c73e834a470c" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411000 1080 "¬P∧¬Q" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411000 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411000 1083 #uuid "07697fb7-beba-509d-9af4-221c0a6b4e35" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411001 1080 "Follow the recipe, and the dish won't be delicious." 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411001 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411001 108