079 #uuid "c0ff40c2-b221-5f65-9fd3-02b3e136bbd5" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411058 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411053 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411058 1069 "q-182493" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411058 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411058 1071 "Simplify the statement $M⇒¬N.$" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411058 1072 #uuid "17e8ba9f-418a-585c-9332-bdf5c1ee589a" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411058 1074 "The conditional statement $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q:$\n\nSo, in this case, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nM⇒¬N≡¬M∨¬N\n$$\n\nUsing De Morgan's law, we reach\n\n$$\n¬M∨¬N≡¬(M∧N).\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $¬(M∧N).$" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411059 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411050 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411059 1069 "q-182589" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411059 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411059 1071 "$\\,$ Win this game, or you won't make it to the playoffs.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411059 1072 #uuid "283e6f42-c6ed-5da0-9085-3d8a16902757" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411059 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ Win this game.\n\n- $B:$ You won’t make it to the playoffs.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't win this game.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you don't win this game, then you won't make it to the playoffs." 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411060 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411049 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411060 1069 "q-182567" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411060 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411060 1071 "$\\,$ If you don't wake up early, then you won't have time for breakfast.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411060 1072 #uuid "442f3c28-f104-5684-ab21-f7ea922bad92" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411060 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You don't wake up early.\n\n- $B:$ You won't have time for breakfast.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You wake up early.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You wake up early, or you won't have time for breakfast.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Wake up early, or you won't have time for breakfast." 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411061 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411051 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411061 1069 "q-102732" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411061 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411061 1071 "Simplify the statement $¬(¬P⇒Q).$" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411061 1072 #uuid "5cc8fa9c-919a-5715-b893-d15125898b55" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411061 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(¬P⇒Q)≡¬(¬(¬P)∨Q).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(¬P)∨Q)≡¬(P∨Q).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can distribute the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law:\n\n$$\n¬(P∨Q)≡¬P∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $¬P∧¬Q.$" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411062 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411052 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411062 1069 "q-182599" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411062 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411062 1071 "$\\,$ Either you do not study for the test, or you will get a good grade.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411062 1072 #uuid "64778f38-4137-5738-8e84-69949fb75847" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411062 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You do not study for the test.\n\n- $B:$ You will get a good grade.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You study for the test.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you study for the test, then you will get a good grade.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ If you study for the test, you will get a good grade.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411063 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411054 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411063 1069 "q-182509" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411063 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411063 1071 "$\\,$ If you follow the recipe, the dish will be delicious.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411063 1072 #uuid "879f4dd5-148a-578f-a2fe-b0846abdf946" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411063 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You follow the recipe.\n\n- $B:$ The dish will be delicious.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You do not follow the recipe.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You do not follow the recipe, or the dish will be delicious.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Either you do not follow the recipe, or the dish will be delicious.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411064 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411048 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411064 1069 "q-182596" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411064 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411064 1071 "$\\,$ Either you do not wear a seatbelt, or you'll be safe.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411064 1072 #uuid "94ae2108-4770-5706-9cb7-823f98b8c863" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411064 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You do not wear a seatbelt.\n\n- $B:$ You'll be safe.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You wear a seatbelt.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you wear a seatbelt, then you'll be safe.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ If you wear a seatbelt, you'll be safe.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411065 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411057 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411065 1069 "q-182503" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411065 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411065 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A⇒B≡¬A∨B$\n- $¬(A⇒B)≡A∨¬B$\n- $¬(A⇒B)≡A∧¬B$" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411065 1072 #uuid "c7ec246a-1c3b-5830-ae08-ed99cbcc20da" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411065 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A⇒B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬A∨B.$\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement II is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nA⇒B≡¬A∨B\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(A⇒B)≡¬(¬A∨B).\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬A∨B)≡¬(¬A)∧¬B.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nA∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(A⇒B)$ is equivalent to $A∧¬B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411056 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411066 1069 "q-182494" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411066 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411066 1071 "Simplify the statement $¬(X⇒Y).$" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411066 1072 #uuid "cb0a268b-ca46-5b91-9359-0e418e71bab9" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true] [17592186411066 1074 "The conditional statement $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q:$\n\nSo, in this case, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\n¬(X⇒Y)≡¬(¬X∨Y)\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬X∨Y)≡¬(¬X)∧¬Y.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nX∧¬Y.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $X∧¬Y.$" 13194139534125 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-031baea5-6ff9-513e-b2ec-02a90e898fe5" 17592186410998 "answer-05f48d79-1191-5d91-bccd-c73e834a470c" 17592186410999 "answer-07697fb7-beba-509d-9af4-221c0a6b4e35" 17592186411000 "answer-087177ce-72c4-5ef2-938f-b10e9e8b40fc" 17592186411001 "answer-0ce79388-e687-51e4-8c79-52bb8958a2df" 17592186411002 "answer-0fbae154-dca8-5832-b6f3-6ca25402e83c" 17592186411003 "answer-151b8ca4-58d5-5c25-9aaa-e1dc70bfd781" 17592186411004 "answer-1b70caa9-c52e-5616-ae35-c8639d9d1cba" 17592186411005 "answer-24af826b-425f-56bf-9262-da3d54c093a3" 17592186411006 "answer-33a5b76d-0c32-5808-9831-19236d7bd6e2" 17592186411007 "answer-47287ee3-35fd-5008-b4df-10ce8e6df1f4" 17592186411008 "answer-4c22c6ff-4399-5df2-b80e-59ddc05f68af" 17592186411009 "answer-50d09d1f-cca0-5966-9603-721e0fd176bc" 17592186411010 "answer-54410627-bf6a-5ebd-8c2f-24cf4377b4c9" 17592186411011 "answer-54794834-147f-5ca2-b3aa-a53057dc42b0" 17592186411012 "answer-5bb25c3e-15c7-56b1-961b-3c21dd8e7b57" 17592186411013 "answer-636ebc41-322a-50d9-8335-010fc61f221b" 17592186411014 "answer-63d8127b-ac0b-521d-8659-2c189d0397b8" 17592186411015 "answer-67bc61ce-a9ad-55ef-865b-df1dad087605" 17592186411016 "answer-79ead6e4-4892-5762-bdb7-b1413f5768d3" 17592186411017 "answer-7ab520b5-59f6-5feb-953d-189ae99c37f8" 17592186411018 "answer-7bffc6ae-a7c2-591c-bc55-a77e46655d6f" 17592186411019 "answer-7d0752bb-bd77-5627-a153-d3ceecd28fb8" 17592186411020 "answer-7f017770-d6b6-5bde-a043-0413160c76ac" 17592186411021 "answer-7f9f9df7-1486-526b-82e1-407e863e4309" 17592186411022 "answer-85a8fd88-3b39-5857-b51b-e87763378f9b" 17592186411023 "answer-88f19e57-12d9-52cf-9408-6a343c734323" 17592186411024 "answer-8ae411bb-3f11-5442-8eba-d9d404fe68e6"